+++ b/docs/nas/keeping-up-with-upstream.md
@@ -17,7 +17,9 @@ Upstream (SteffenLav/qmx-panadapter) releases often, and each release is a movin
 | v1.26.3 (2026-11-13) | Nothing: the series applied as it was | - |  |
 | v2.0.0 (2026-11-15) | Upstream raised DIRTY_WORDS to 6 itself and took bit 160 for RTTY; /api/status gained rtty_mark_off where the NAS adds radio_link; set_frequency() became set_frequency_from(); upstream’s apply_esp_hosted_sdio_recovery.ps1 now re-runs the other sdio patches with a command named "powershell", which macOS does not have; RTTY transmit is new; the NAS’s own sdio PSRAM-copy script carried a regex with literal line ends that missed on a CRLF checkout | DIRTY_RELAY 161; both status fields kept (0001); the refused-frequency check moved into set_frequency_from() (0039); the recovery script runs the other patches with the PowerShell already running it, pwsh on the Mac (0044); RTTY refuses to transmit while another device has the transmitter (0045); the regex uses \r?\n (0004) |  |
 | v2.0.2 (2026-11-18) | Upstream took bit 161 for the RTTY contest settings (R030) and bit 162 for the TX subnet interlock (T206); the README’s licence section, which the NAS appendix follows, gained the MP3-decoder licence note; the web radio’s Helix MP3 component was replaced by minimp3 vendored in the tree | DIRTY_RELAY 163 (28 free) (0001); the appendix kept after upstream’s new licence text (0041); nothing to carry for the MP3 decoder. Upstream’s new check_tx_guard passes on the NAS version |  |
+| v2.1.0 (2026-11-22) | No conflicting change, but git am stopped at 0029: the series had grown on the Mac on top of v2.0.2 (0047-0052), and a patch made on top of earlier ones names README blobs that only exist with v2.0.2 underneath, so git am could not build its three-way base ("sha1 information is lacking"). Upstream also moved every "did not transmit" refusal to a red alert toast | The series was rebased onto v2.1.0 (it merged without conflicts) and SERIES_BASE is v2.1.0; the "TX locked" refusals use the red alert toast too (0053). Settings bits and drawer sections unchanged (DIRTY_RELAY 163, sections 57-59) |  |
+| v2.1.2 (2026-11-26) | Upstream took settings bits 163-168 (spectrum/waterfall split height, FT8/FT4 receive band hopping, TXCQ period, hop CQ split, WSPR hop stay), so DIRTY_RELAY 163 now collided; drawer section 57 (FT8/FT4 band hopping); psk_rx.c's network check became three lines with a reason ("WiFi not connected"); ft8_tx.c gained a wait for the radio to confirm a band hop before key-down, next to the TX-locked check | DIRTY_RELAY 169 (22 free) (0001); Radio source section 58, Power off the radio 59, Power on the radio 60, N_DRAWER_SECTIONS 61 (0005, 0049, 0050); psk_rx.c keeps upstream's reasons and says "network not connected" when neither Ethernet nor Wi-Fi is up (0040); the TX-locked refusal sits after the band-hop check (0029) |  |
 
 *Each port is checked the same way: a full ESP-IDF v5.4.4 build with every source compiled and the standing patches applied, upstream’s check scripts (check_dirty_bits, check_tx_guard, check_unattended_tx, check_web, check_webui, check_docs) and host harnesses run on plain upstream and on the NAS version with the same results, and the new kit run against GitHub on the Mac, producing the same tree. The relay’s discovery fix (0042, 2026-10-31) was not a port: it was found on the NAS when a VPN client was connected (section 3).*
 
